mode: "100644"
text: "# The reality of Synthesia in 2026 (verify-quarterly)\n\nTiers, gates, and engine versions shift — attribute, **verify-quarterly** at synthesia.io and\ndocs.synthesia.io; check feature gates in-app before committing.\n\n## What it is (and where it honestly wins/loses)\nSynthesia is the **enterprise avatar-video category leader** (used by 90%+ of the Fortune 100 per its own\npositioning; attribute): script → a consented AI presenter speaking it in **140–160+ languages** with 1,000+\nvoices. **Where it wins:** multilingual training/L&D, onboarding, compliance, explainers, localization at\nscale — one script, dozens of languages, no re-shoots, a presenter who never has a bad day. **Where it loses\n(reviews converge):** avatars read polished-but-**clinical** — they lag real presenters on emotional nuance,\nwhich makes them wrong for trust-led founder content, testimonials, and emotionally-driven marketing (route\nthose to `talking-head-and-piece-to-camera`); HeyGen-style tools read more native for casual TikTok content.\n\"Not for you if\" (attribute max-productive): solo creators under ~3–4 videos/month, regularly-regulated\nmedical/pharma topics, cinematic brand storytelling.\n\n## Synthesia 3.0 (Oct 2025) + the 2026 stack (attribute)\n**Express-2 engine** — full-body movement, hand gestures, micro-expressions, 1080p/30fps, no length cap ·\n**Video Agents** — real-time conversational avatars (Enterprise) · **AI Playground** — embedded B-roll\ninside the editor (launched with Sora 2 + Veo 3.1; OpenAI's Sora API sunsets Sept 24 2026 — treat the\nVeo lane as the durable one) · Interactivity 2.0 (quizzes/polls/hotspots), Courses, **AI Dubbing**, Copilot\n· AI Video Assistant (doc/PPT/URL → drafted video) · SSML support · rendering ≈ real-time (a 1-min video in\n~1–2 min) · subtitles in 39+ languages.\n\n## The consent architecture (the industry benchmark — from Synthesia's own docs)\n- **Stock avatars** (240+) are built from **real, paid, consenting actors.**\n- **Personal Avatars** require **identity verification via a live consent recording** — the person consenting\n  must be the person in the source media; source video must be a **single continuous take (1–5 min), never\n  spliced or manipulated**; photo avatars: well-lit, waist-up, neutral background, teeth visible (lip-sync).\n- **No celebrities/politicians without authorization; deepfakes, misinformation, and impersonation\n  prohibited.** Moderation = AI + human. Sharing governance: outfits/spaces shareable, recipients can use but\n  not edit; the voice is shared separately.\n- Certifications: SOC 2 Type II, GDPR, ISO 42001, ISO 27701; Content Authenticity Initiative membership\n  (Enterprise); customer data not used for base-model training without written approval.\n- The honest cost of that governance: **moderation over-flags legitimate content** — healthcare/pharma teams\n  report 12–24h manual-review delays (attribute).\n\n## Tiers + the traps (≈; verify in-app)\n**Free** 10 min/mo, watermark, ~9 avatars · **Starter ≈ $18/mo annual ($29 monthly)** — ~120 min/yr, 125+\navatars · **Creator ≈ $64/mo annual ($89)** — ~360 min/yr, 180+ avatars, **API**, interactive video, voice\ncloning · **Enterprise** custom (unlimited minutes, full library, **SCORM + 1-click translation + Video\nAgents**, SSO). **Traps:** minutes/credits **don't roll over**; largely **non-refundable annual** terms;\ncustom/Studio avatars ≈ **$1,000/yr per avatar** (~10-day processing); **minor script edits force a full\nre-generation (~8–12 min)** that eats minute caps — lock copy before generating; reported render/audio-sync\nfailures near deadlines — build buffer.\n\n## House metrics\nCompletion/watch-through on the published videos + training outcomes (LMS) — native only; never fabricated.\n"
